+## Reflection
+
+**One survey verdict was wrong on reading, and it is the one the order predicted.** The verdict archived `omegahive_hive_user_migration.md` as an "executed procedure." Its own revision record says otherwise: Parts A–C (additive prep) were executed live on Beastie Jul 27 2026 and the document was corrected from that run, but **Part D — the cutover — is written and deliberately unexecuted**, and Part E's follow-ups wait on it. Three more things corroborate: it is item 2 of the Phase 2 identity gate, so it is open gate work; `omegahive_recovery_runbook.md` §6 routes the operator to it as *the* procedure for an account change; and `deploy/hive-user/precheck.sh` and `restore_rehearsal.sh` cite it as live tooling. Per the order's conservative-on-doubt rule it **stays in `docs/`**, labelled "CURRENT — procedure partly executed, cutover pending," with the archive trigger written into the label: it moves when Part D lands. Filing it as executed history would have buried a procedure with a scheduled future.
+
+**The order triages 14 docs; `docs/` had 22.** The eight the survey never staged are all self-contained work orders — `v0b_order`, `c2_v0a_v2_order`, `v3_kb_persona_brief`, `stage2_fixes_A1-A4`, `port_fixes_D1-D5`, `v3_fixes_B1-B6`, plus `o2_fork_patches` and `c2_battery_spec`. Several are demonstrably closed (the port fixes and stage-2 fixes describe merged commits; the fork patches produced the hive image). Under "moves and labels only, conservative on doubt" they stay in place, but each now carries an INDEX line that says so explicitly — *"Work order, on the front path — not triaged by the Jul 28 2026 docs pass"* — so the gap is visible rather than silent. **This is the next docs pass's work, and it is bigger than what this one did**: a `docs/orders/` shelf (or archiving the executed ones) would take the front path from 16 files to about 9. That is the shelf decision this triage did not have authority to make.
+
+**A wrinkle worth naming for whoever writes that order.** Three of those closed work orders cite `omegahive_stage2_spec.md` as their contract, and it is now archived. Nothing breaks — the links resolve into `docs/archive/`, and archive means retained, not deleted — but it does mean a current-shelf doc pointing at an archived one, which reads as inconsistent until the work orders are themselves shelved. Archiving `stage2_spec` was still right: the ladder is closed, `ladder/README.md` says so, and the verdict governs.
+
+**One deliberate non-edit.** `docs/archive/omegahive_docs_audit.md` mentions `omegahive_omegaclaw_binding.md` bare, in a Jun 29 2026 table whose whole point is to record each doc's status *at that date* ("**current** | none — just written"). Repointing a path inside a dated snapshot would falsify the snapshot. Left as is; the INDEX labels the audit a historical snapshot.
+
+**What consumed the effort** — not the moves. Six `git mv`s and the link repoints took one pass and were mechanical, exactly as predicted. The spend was **reading every doc's header and revision record to write its INDEX line honestly**, and the reading is what produced the only two findings of the task: the migration-doc deviation and the 13 pre-existing archive danglers. An INDEX that says "what it is, its authority scope, its status" cannot be assembled from filenames — the authority-scope clause in particular forced a judgment per doc (normative? historical? both, and which part is which?). If a future order wants this cheaper, the lever is not faster moves; it is per-doc Status headers that already answer the authority-scope question, which is what this pass has now put in place for the 22 files it touched or indexed.
+
+**Effort, measured:** first branch commit `3ff8418` to merge commit `6186a46` spans **under five minutes of commit timestamps**, which is the only clock this session can point at and is *not* the session's real span — most of the work (reading the docs, validating the verdicts) happened before the first commit, and no timestamp records it. Reported here as what consumed it, not as a duration.
